  {
    id: 'sf2',
    title: '高效复习的五个做法',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 9,
    text: "Most students believe that a long evening at a desk is a successful evening. ___36___ Real learning shows itself only when you can produce the answer without looking at the page. The first rule is to test before you read. Close the book, take a blank card and write down everything you remember about one small topic. ___37___ The missing part is exactly what you should read next, and it is usually far shorter than the chapter you feared. The second rule is to space the work. Reviewing a text on the third day and the seventh day keeps it alive with two short looks; reviewing it every night needs fourteen looks and often works less well. ___38___ A calendar with four subjects in it beats a plan that says only I will study hard. The third rule concerns rest. During sleep the brain repeats what the day placed before it, which is why a tired mind makes poor notes and a rested mind finds the right words. ___39___ Phone, message and video belong outside the study hour, because every small interruption costs two minutes of return. Finally, speak what you have learned to another person. If you cannot explain a rule in simple sentences, you have memorised the shape of it rather than the meaning, and the exam will ask for the meaning. ___40___ Two honest hours are worth more than five tired ones, and no clever plan can take the place of a real attempt to remember.",
    options: [
      { k: 'A', en: 'Choose your fixed evenings first, then mark the short reviews on those days.' },
      { k: 'B', en: 'Read the chapter once more from beginning to end and underline the new words.' },
      { k: 'C', en: 'This habit is easy to build and needs no expensive tool.' },
      { k: 'D', en: 'Students who study late often feel hard work has been done.' },
      { k: 'E', en: 'Check your list against the book only after the card is full.' },
      { k: 'F', en: 'A short walk before study also prepares the mind for new ideas.' },
      { k: 'G', en: 'So a night without sleep can quietly remove the value of a whole week.' },
    ],
    answers: [
      { g: '36', k: 'D', explain: '词汇复现与段落主旨：前句说学生把久坐当作高效，D 承接这种错觉，后句 Real learning… 构成对照。' },
      { g: '37', k: 'E', explain: '代词指代与衔接：前句让你先默写卡片，E 说写满之后再对照书本，才引出后句 the missing part。' },
      { g: '38', k: 'A', explain: '段落主旨与词汇复现：本段讲分散复习，A 的 fixed evenings 与 short reviews 正对应两次的回头看，并与后句 a calendar with four subjects 呼应。' },
      { g: '39', k: 'G', explain: '逻辑关系（因果）：前句解释睡眠中大脑会重演白天所学，G 用 so 推出缺觉会毁掉一周的复习，后句接着讲别打断。' },
      { g: '40', k: 'C', explain: '代词指代：上文最后一招是把学到的讲给别人，C 用 this habit 收束这一做法，再接末句两个诚实的小时。' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'sf3',
    title: '参加社团的价值',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 9,
    text: 'A school club is often treated as a pleasant extra, something to drop when the exams come near. ___36___ In truth, the club is one of the few places where a student must run a real task without a teacher standing beside him. Consider the first thing a club teaches: the cost of a promise. When you agree to bring the chairs or to write the weekly note, someone else depends on that small act. ___37___ A club also puts you among people with different strengths: one draws the poster, one asks the teachers for a room, and one keeps the money. Nobody can finish a show without the others. ___38___ Later, when work asks a team to plan an event, the habit of sharing has already been learned. Every club also teaches a harder lesson about failure. A play may open to twelve people because someone forgot the date. ___39___ In a classroom a bad mark is easy to hide; in a club the result stands in front of everyone, and the only answer is to come back next week and try a different way. That return is not confidence but knowledge built from small repeated attempts. Some parents worry that club hours take hours from study. The worry is fair, since a term has a fixed number of evenings. ___40___ A student who has run one project plans his reading better, because he has felt what a deadline means. Choose one club, stay until its work ends, and let the exams have the last two weeks.',
    options: [
      { k: 'A', en: 'That is why a club year can matter more than a marked exercise book.' },
      { k: 'B', en: 'Missing it costs other people, not only yourself.' },
      { k: 'C', en: 'Clubs usually ask members to pay a small yearly fee for materials.' },
      { k: 'D', en: 'Few schools explain to students what the club is actually for.' },
      { k: 'E', en: 'But a club can also make a weak subject feel hopeless.' },
      { k: 'F', en: 'The answer is a limit rather than a refusal.' },
      { k: 'G', en: 'Nobody remembers the date, and the group learns that details are shared work.' },
    ],
    answers: [
      { g: '36', k: 'D', explain: '词汇复现与段落主旨：前句说社团被当成可有可无的“额外”，D 指出学校很少说明社团究竟为何存在，后句 In truth 给出回答。' },
      { g: '37', k: 'B', explain: '代词指代：B 的 it 指前句那个小小的承诺，接住 someone else depends on that small act 这一层代价。' },
      { g: '38', k: 'A', explain: '段落主旨与衔接词：本段讲分工，A 用 that is why 收束，并与后句 Later, when work asks a team… 顺承。' },
      { g: '39', k: 'G', explain: '词汇复现：前句说演出冷清是因为有人忘了日期，G 复现 date 并落到 details are shared work，与后句 in front of everyone 相接。' },
      { g: '40', k: 'F', explain: '逻辑关系（转折后的回应）：前文承认家长的担心有道理，F 说答案是“设限”而非“拒绝”，直接引出后句 let the exams have the last two weeks。' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'sf4',
    title: '与父母沟通的四件小事',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 9,
    text: 'Almost every family argues about the same few things: study time, phone time and sleep. ___36___ The first small thing is the hour you choose. A serious question asked while your mother is washing the dishes has already lost, because her attention is only half there. Wait for a quiet ten minutes, sit down, and say what you want in one clear sentence. The second thing is the length of your explanation. Long speeches make parents defensive, since they hear an accusation rather than a request. ___37___ Three honest sentences are stronger than fifteen, and a short one cannot be argued with word by word. The third thing is the habit of asking for their opinion before you need it. If you consult your father about money in an ordinary month, he will feel trusted rather than attacked when the real problem arrives. ___38___ This is the part most students skip, and it is the part that changes hearts. The fourth thing is what happens when you are wrong. Everybody forgets a promise, and the first answer is often a small excuse. ___39___ Saying “I was wrong, and here is how I will fix it” ends an argument in a minute, while a defence usually doubles it. None of these four things is easy, and none of them guarantees success. Parents also have bad days, fixed ideas and their own tiredness. ___40___ Over a year a family can build a shared language in which a short sentence means real respect.',
    options: [
      { k: 'A', en: 'They are habits rather than tricks, and habits need repetition.' },
      { k: 'B', en: 'A question about a school trip is not the same as a request for freedom.' },
      { k: 'C', en: 'Most of these arguments begin because both sides are tired and nobody feels heard.' },
      { k: 'D', en: 'Say what you need, and stop there.' },
      { k: 'E', en: 'Admitting it at once is far cheaper than hiding it.' },
      { k: 'F', en: 'Parents often worry about their own work when they ask about your grades.' },
      { k: 'G', en: 'Trust grows slowly, like a plant, and needs the same small care every week.' },
    ],
    answers: [
      { g: '36', k: 'C', explain: '段落主旨与词汇复现：前句列出家里反复争吵的几件事，C 解释起因（双方都累、没人被听见），开启下文四条建议。' },
      { g: '37', k: 'D', explain: '段落主旨与衔接：本段讲解释要短，D 用祈使句“说清需要就停”，紧接后句 Three honest sentences are stronger than fifteen。' },
      { g: '38', k: 'G', explain: '代词指代与逻辑关系：前句说平时无所谓地请教会让父母感到被信任，G 用比喻说明信任慢慢生长，后句 this is the part 正指它。' },
      { g: '39', k: 'E', explain: '衔接词与对比：前句说人常找小借口，E 说立刻承认代价更小，与后句 Saying I was wrong… 构成同一主张的展开。' },
      { g: '40', k: 'A', explain: '段落主旨：前句承认这四件事并不保证成功，A 说明它们是习惯而非巧计，需要重复，接住末句 Over a year。' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'sf5',
    title: '使用电子设备的边界',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 9,
    text: 'A phone in the schoolbag is now as ordinary as a pencil case, and the argument never stops. Schools that ban it completely find the devices in pockets; schools that allow it everywhere see attention fall apart. ___36___ The first line is time. A student needs one working hour without a screen and a night with enough sleep, since a phone on the pillow invites one more look. ___37___ The second line is place. A device at the dinner table turns a family hour into separate small rooms, and a runner who watches a screen never really runs. Some spaces should stay free, and choosing them is easier than policing all day. The third line is purpose. It is honest to say why you open a page: work, a message to a friend, or simple boredom. ___38___ Boredom handled by a screen grows faster than boredom handled by a walk, and a student who names the reason keeps control. A boundary also needs a voice outside the family. Ask a classmate or a PE teacher how many hours they really use, and the numbers often surprise you. ___39___ A limit set alone feels like a punishment; a limit agreed with others becomes a plan. Finally, expect a bad week. The first nights without a late scroll are difficult, and the fourth day brings an excuse to break the rule. ___40___ A boundary is not a wall; it is a line that you can step back from when you cross it, and that you cross on purpose sometimes.',
    options: [
      { k: 'A', en: 'That is why a simple ban solves very little on its own.' },
      { k: 'B', en: 'A fixed hour before the exam is a better plan than a long open one.' },
      { k: 'C', en: 'For many families the useful answer is a set of clear lines.' },
      { k: 'D', en: 'Screens should never be allowed in any classroom, whatever the age.' },
      { k: 'E', en: 'A rule that no one knows about cannot be broken or kept.' },
      { k: 'F', en: 'Write the reason in three words before the page opens.' },
      { k: 'G', en: 'Recovery matters more than the wish to be strict forever.' },
    ],
    answers: [
      { g: '36', k: 'C', explain: '逻辑关系与段落主旨：前文说完全禁止与完全放开都失败，C 顺势提出“几条清楚的线”，统领下文三条界线。' },
      { g: '37', k: 'B', explain: '词汇复现（time、hour、plan）：本段讲时间这条线，B 给出考试前的固定一小时，与前后句 sleep、one more look 同向。' },
      { g: '38', k: 'F', explain: '代词指代：前句说要说出打开页面的理由，F 把 reason 压缩成三个词写在打开之前，后句 boredom 接着展开。' },
      { g: '39', k: 'E', explain: '衔接与词汇复现：前句说和别人谈谈真实使用时长，E 说没人知道的规则谈不上遵守，引出后句 agreed with others。' },
      { g: '40', k: 'G', explain: '段落主旨：前句承认第一个月会有人破例，G 强调恢复比永远严格更重要，与末句 step back from 一致。' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'sf6',
    title: '考砸之后该做什么',
    exam: '中考',
    mins: 8,
    text: 'A bad grade can hurt more than a hard question. You look at the paper, see a low number, and a small voice says that you are not clever. ___36___ The first day is for feelings, not for work. Close the paper and go outside for twenty minutes. Talk to one person you trust, and say only one line: I am upset about this. ___37___ Do not study hard on that night. Sleep well, and let the first wave pass. The next step is to read the paper with a calm mind. Take a pencil and circle every mistake. Count the circles in each part, and write the small numbers on the top of the page. ___38___ Now the problem has a size, and a thing with a size can be worked on. Then ask one clear question to one teacher: What should I fix first? ___39___ Do not try to fix eight things in one week; pick two small ones and keep them in front of you for ten days. The last step is the hardest. You must do the same kind of work again, and you must look at the result without hiding the paper. This is the point where many people stop, because a second bad result feels like proof. ___40___ Failure is not a sign that you should stop; it is information about what to do next.',
    options: [
      { k: 'A', en: 'One sad talk is enough for one day.' },
      { k: 'B', en: 'Those numbers show you where the real problem is.' },
      { k: 'C', en: 'A short list is far better than a long wish.' },
      { k: 'D', en: 'That voice is lying, and you can learn to send it away.' },
      { k: 'E', en: 'You should do all your homework again from the first page.' },
      { k: 'F', en: 'But doing it once more is the only way to grow.' },
      { k: 'G', en: 'Every parent wants their child to pass the exam.' },
    ],
    answers: [
      { g: '36', k: 'D', explain: '代词指代：前句的 a small voice 被 D 接住，说它在骗你，你可以学着把它送走，下文进入具体步骤。' },
      { g: '37', k: 'A', explain: '段落主旨与词汇复现：本段讲第一天只处理心情，A 收束“一天一次难过的谈话就够了”，后句 Do not study hard 紧接。' },
      { g: '38', k: 'B', explain: '衔接词与词汇复现：前句让你在各部分写上小数字，B 用 those numbers 指代它们，说明问题有了大小。' },
      { g: '39', k: 'C', explain: '词汇复现与逻辑关系：前句只问老师一个问题，C 说短清单胜过长期望，引出后句 pick two small ones。' },
      { g: '40', k: 'F', explain: '衔接词（转折）：前句说很多人怕第二次失败就停下，F 用 but 指出再做一次才是唯一的办法，与末句对失败的定义相接。' },
    ],
  },
